<https://example.org/dmo/id/adverseEffect/euglycaemic-dka> a dmo:AdverseEffect ;
    rdfs:label "Euglycaemic DKA" ;
    dmo:adverseEffectCode "euglycaemic-dka" ;
    dmo:frequencyCategory "Unknown" ;
    dmo:name "Euglycaemic DKA" ;
    dmo:severity "Unknown" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "With the widespread use of the sodium-glucose cotransporter (SGLT) inhibitor class of drugs (e.g. dapagliflozin, canagliflozin, empagliflozin, ertugliflozin, sotagliflozin) people with type 2 diabetes, and increasingly in those with type 1, has highlighted the importance of using pH and ketones (rather than the older ‘glucose-centric’ care) to guide the diagnosis and management. This is because of the risk of developing euglycaemic DKA with these agents (7)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/adverseEffect/hypoglycaemia> a dmo:AdverseEffect ;
    rdfs:label "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:adverseEffectCode "hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:frequencyCategory "Uncommon" ;
    dmo:name "Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:severity "Unknown" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "hypoglycaemia and hypokalaemia were not infrequent if the infusion rate was kept at 0.1 unit/kg/hr ever though 10% glucose had been commenced." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "When the glucose concentrations drops to ≤14.0 mmol/L consider reducing the rate of intravenous insulin infusion to 0.05 units/kg/hour to reduce the risk of developing hypoglycaemia and / or hypokalaemia" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "A national survey of DKA management following earlier version of this guideline found that the rates of hypoglycaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) and hypokalaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) were 27.6% and 67% respectively." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

