<https://example.org/dmo/id/complication/diabetic-ketoacidosis> a dmo:Complication ;
    rdfs:label "Diabetic Ketoacidosis" ;
    dmo:affectedOrgan "Systemic" ;
    dmo:complicationCode "diabetic-ketoacidosis" ;
    dmo:definition "The management of diabetic ketoacidosis in adults" ;
    dmo:isEmergency "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005147" ;
    dmo:name "Diabetic Ketoacidosis" ;
    dmo:onsetPattern "Acute" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The management of diabetic ketoacidosis in adults" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is a frequent and potentially life-threatening complication of type 1 diabetes." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "he care of people with diabetes who suffer this life threating complication and for their commitment to continuing to update the guidelines in the light of further evidence." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Aim: • Ensure that clinical and biochemical parameters are continuing to improve or are normal" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "People who present with recurrent episodes of DKA comprise a significant proportion of all DKA admissions – in the UK accounting for 66% of those with type 1 diabetes and 35% of those with type 2 (24)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "A national survey of DKA management following earlier version of this guideline found that the rates of hypoglycaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) and hypokalaemia (<4.0 mmol/L) were 27.6% and 67% respectively." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Diabetic ketoacidosis is a medical emergency requiring prompt treatment, and is different to a ketosis of pregnancy." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "DKA pathway of care\n\n25\nT = 0 at time intravenous fluids are commenced. If there is a problem with intravenous access, critical care support should be requested immediately." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Hypokalaemia and hyperkalaemia are life threatening conditions and are common in DKA." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Resolution of DKA is defined as ketones less than 0.6 mmol/L and venous pH over 7.3" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is a complex disordered metabolic state characterised by hyperglycaemia, ketonaemia, and acidosis." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Diabetic ketoacidosis is a common medical emergency and must be treated appropriately." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "We recommend that every Acute Trust use performance indicators to assess the quality of care given (examples given in Appendix 2). A Treatment Pathway document may be beneficial, as adherence to guidelines for this condition is very poor and integrated pathway documents (an example of which is given online) would improve compliance" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Diabetic ketoacidosis in people on maintenance haemodialysis: case reports and review of literature." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The person’s previous regimen should generally be re-started if their most recent HbA1c suggests acceptable level of control i.e. HbA1c 64 mmol/mmol (<8.0%)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "People admitted to hospital with diabetic ketoacidosis should be referred to the diabetes specialist team on admission" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "DKA: CAPILLARY BLOOD KETONES < 0.6 mmol/L and HCO3 > 15 mmol/L and STILL not eating and drinking" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "ADULT DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (DKA) – MANAGEMENT CHART" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

