<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/diabetic-ketoacidosis-in-pregnancy> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Diabetic ketoacidosis in pregnancy" ;
    dmo:definition "Diabetic ketoacidosis is a medical emergency requiring prompt treatment, and is different to a ketosis of pregnancy. DKA in pregnancy is an obstetric emergency as it carries a risk of mortality and morbidity for the pregnant woman and the fetus (59)." ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "diabetic-ketoacidosis-in-pregnancy" ;
    dmo:name "Diabetic ketoacidosis in pregnancy" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Diabetic ketoacidosis is a medical emergency requiring prompt treatment, and is different to a ketosis of pregnancy. DKA in pregnancy is an obstetric emergency as it carries a risk of mortality and morbidity for the pregnant woman and the fetus (59)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/euglycaemic-dka> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Euglycaemic DKA" ;
    dmo:definition "DKA in pregnancy can occur with only very modest elevation of glucose levels, i.e. <13.9mmol/l, (‘euglycaemic DKA’) in women during pregnancy." ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "euglycaemic-dka" ;
    dmo:name "Euglycaemic DKA" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "DKA in pregnancy can occur with only very modest elevation of glucose levels, i.e. <13.9mmol/l,  (‘euglycaemic DKA’) in women during pregnancy." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/ketosis-prone-type-2-diabetes> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Ketosis Prone Type 2 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:definition "This guidance also addresses the new problem of DKA and particularly euglycaemic DKA in those treated with SGLT-2 inhibitors. It also for the first time considers ketosis prone type 2 diabetes and the complex issue of the management of DKA in people with end stage renal failure or on dialysis." ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "ketosis-prone-type-2-diabetes" ;
    dmo:insulinRequired "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:name "Ketosis Prone Type 2 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:prevalenceShare "0.05"^^xsd:decimal ;
    dmo:typicalOnset "Adulthood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "It also for the first time considers ketosis prone type 2 diabetes and the complex issue of the management of DKA in people with end stage renal failure or on dialysis." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "DKA does not exclusively occur in people with type 1 diabetes, and people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA – so called ‘ketosis prone type 2 diabetes’ (11)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA – so called ‘ketosis prone type 2 diabetes’ (11). This most often occurs in people of Afro-Caribbean or Hispanic descent." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-1-diabetes> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Type 1 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:definition "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is a frequent and potentially life-threatening complication of type 1 diabetes." ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "type-1-diabetes" ;
    dmo:insulinRequired "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005147" ;
    dmo:name "Type 1 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:prevalenceShare "0.1"^^xsd:decimal ;
    dmo:typicalOnset "Childhood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is a frequent and potentially life-threatening complication of type 1 diabetes." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "DKA does not exclusively occur in people with type 1 diabetes, and people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA – so called ‘ketosis prone type 2 diabetes’ (11)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "occur in people with type 1 diabetes, and people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "People who present with recurrent episodes of DKA comprise a significant proportion of all DKA admissions – in the UK accounting for 66% of those with type 1 diabetes and 35% of those with type 2 (24)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Those presenting with newly diagnosed type 1 diabetes should be given long acting basal insulin (e.g. glargine, detemir or degludec - or human NPH insulin, depending on local policy) at a dose of 0.25 units/Kg subcutaneously once daily to mitigate against rebound ketosis when they are taken off the FRIII (68)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "DKA usually occurs as a consequence of absolute or relative insulin deficiency that is accompanied by an increase in counter regulatory hormones (i.e., glucagon, cortisol, growth hormone, catecholamines)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "In the majority of cases people with type 1 diabetes should be under specialist care." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Trends in hospital admission for diabetic ketoacidosis in adults with type 1 and type 2 diabetes in England, 1998-2013: A retrospective cohort study." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Comparison of once- versus twice-daily administration of insulin detemir, used with mealtime insulin aspart, in basal-bolus therapy for type 1 diabetes: Assessment of detemir administration in a progressive treat-to-target trial (ADAPT)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-2-diabetes> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Type 2 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:definition "DKA does not exclusively occur in people with type 1 diabetes, and people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA – so called ‘ketosis prone type 2 diabetes’ (11)." ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "type-2-diabetes" ;
    dmo:insulinRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005148" ;
    dmo:name "Type 2 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:prevalenceShare "0.9"^^xsd:decimal ;
    dmo:typicalOnset "Adulthood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "DKA does not exclusively occur in people with type 1 diabetes, and people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA – so called ‘ketosis prone type 2 diabetes’ (11)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "people with type 2 diabetes may also develop DKA – so called ‘ketosis prone type 2 diabetes’" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "People who present with recurrent episodes of DKA comprise a significant proportion of all DKA admissions – in the UK accounting for 66% of those with type 1 diabetes and 35% of those with type 2 (24)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Trends in hospital admission for diabetic ketoacidosis in adults with type 1 and type 2 diabetes in England, 1998-2013: A retrospective cohort study." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/basal-bolus-insulin-therapy> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Basal Bolus Insulin Therapy" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "basal-bolus-insulin-therapy" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Provides background (basal) insulin and mealtime (bolus) insulin to mimic normal insulin secretion in people with type 1 diabetes." ;
    dmo:name "Basal Bolus Insulin Therapy" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If they were on basal bolus insulin • There should be an overlap between the insulin infusion and first injection of fast acting insulin." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/continuous-subcutaneous-insulin-infusion-csii> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion (CSII)" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "continuous-subcutaneous-insulin-infusion-csii" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Delivers insulin continuously via a pump, providing basal insulin and allowing for mealtime boluses." ;
    dmo:name "Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion (CSII)" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If they were on CSII • Ensure the availability of necessary supplies/ and or consumables" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/fixed-rate-intravenous-insulin-infusion> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed Rate Intravenous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AE01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "fixed-rate-intravenous-insulin-infusion" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin lowers blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake in muscle and fat tissue and by suppressing hepatic glucose production." ;
    dmo:name "Fixed Rate Intravenous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "In these situations admission to a Level 2 / HDU facility should be considered. Fluids should be replaced cautiously." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AE01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin lowers blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake into cells and inhibiting glucose production in the liver." ;
    dmo:name "Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Thus, when glucose drops below 14 mmol/L, consider reducing the rate of intravenous insulin infusion to 0.05 units/kg/hr. This is already an option in the adult guidelines elsewhere (25), and several paediatric studies have suggested that the rate of resolution of DKA is not longer compared to 0.1 units/kg/hr (30-32). It is thus also included in the UK paediatric guidelines (33)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The main driver for hypoglycaemia is the use of insulin. Thus, in addition to commencing 10% dextrose to run alongside the 0.9% sodium chloride solution, when glucose drops below 14 mmol/L, consider reducing the rate of intravenous insulin infusion to 0.05 units/kg/hr." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "ALWAYS continue subcutaneous intermediate* or basal insulin** *Intermediate: Insulatard®, Humulin I®, Insuman Basal® **Basal: Lantus® (glargine), Levemir® (detemir), Tresiba® (degludec), Toujeo® (long acting glargine)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "adolescence, pregnancy (risk of cerebral and pulmonary oedema)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/intravenous-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Intravenous Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "intravenous-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin administered intravenously to lower blood glucose levels in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) and hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state (HHS)." ;
    dmo:name "Intravenous Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "SWITCH FROM FIXED RATE INTRAVENOUS INSULIN INFUSION TO VARIABLE RATE INTRAVENOUS INSULIN INFUSION (VRIII) with 10% Dextrose with 0.15% KCl at 50 mls/hr IF: DKA: CAPILLARY BLOOD KETONES < 0.6 mmol/L and HCO3 > 15 mmol/L and STILL not eating and drinking" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/intravenous-insulin-infusion> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Intravenous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "intravenous-insulin-infusion" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin reduces blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake in peripheral tissues and inhibiting hepatic glucose production." ;
    dmo:name "Intravenous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Continue FRIII until ketones less than 0.6 mmol/L, venous pH >7.3 and/or venous bicarbonate over 18 mmol/L" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/long-acting-basal-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Long Acting Basal Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10BG02" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "long-acting-basal-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "Moderate" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Provides a steady, prolonged release of insulin to manage blood glucose levels throughout the day." ;
    dmo:name "Long Acting Basal Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "then a long acting basal insulin should be commenced, at a dose of 0.25 units/Kg subcutaneously once daily." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Those presenting with newly diagnosed type 1 diabetes should be given long acting basal insulin (e.g. glargine, detemir or degludec - or human NPH insulin, depending on local policy) at a dose of 0.25 units/Kg subcutaneously once daily to mitigate against rebound ketosis when they are taken off the FRIII (68)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/long-acting-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Long Acting Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "long-acting-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Provides a steady, prolonged release of insulin to maintain background (basal) insulin levels over 24 hours." ;
    dmo:name "Long Acting Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If the person was previously on a long acting insulin such as glargine, degludec, detemir or human isophane, this should have been continued" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/rapid-acting-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Rapid Acting Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "rapid-acting-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Acts quickly to cover mealtime glucose spikes, typically starting within 15 minutes and peaking within 1 hour." ;
    dmo:name "Rapid Acting Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The fast acting insulin should be injected with the meal and the intravenous insulin and fluids discontinued 30 to 60 minutes later" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/sodium-glucose-cotransporter-sglt-inhibitor-class-of-drugs> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter (SGLT) Inhibitor Class of Drugs" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "sodium-glucose-cotransporter-sglt-inhibitor-class-of-drugs" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "Low" ;
    dmo:mechanism "SGLT inhibitors promote glucose excretion in the urine by inhibiting the sodium-glucose cotransporter in the kidneys." ;
    dmo:name "Sodium-Glucose Cotransporter (SGLT) Inhibitor Class of Drugs" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Loss" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "With the widespread use of the sodium-glucose cotransporter (SGLT) inhibitor class of drugs (e.g. dapagliflozin, canagliflozin, empagliflozin, ertugliflozin, sotagliflozin) people with type 2 diabetes, and increasingly in those with type 1, has highlighted the importance of using pH and ketones (rather than the older ‘glucose-centric’ care) to guide the diagnosis and management. This is because of the risk of developing euglycaemic DKA with these agents (7)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/twice-daily-fixed-mix-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Twice Daily Fixed-Mix Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "twice-daily-fixed-mix-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Combines short-acting and intermediate-acting insulin in a single injection, given twice daily to provide both basal and bolus insulin coverage." ;
    dmo:name "Twice Daily Fixed-Mix Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If they were on twice daily fixed-mix insulin • Re-introduce the subcutaneous insulin before breakfast or before the evening meal." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/3-beta-hydroxybutyrate> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "3-beta-hydroxybutyrate" ;
    dmo:definition "the main blood ketone" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "3-beta-hydroxybutyrate" ;
    dmo:name "3-beta-hydroxybutyrate" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "portable ketone meters now also allow point of care testing of 3-beta-hydroxybutyrate, the main blood ketone." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/anion-gap> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Anion Gap" ;
    dmo:definition "A measure used to assess the balance of electrolytes in the blood, which was not significantly different in people with end stage renal disease compared to those with preserved renal function." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "anion-gap" ;
    dmo:name "Anion Gap" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Bicarbonate and pH were not significantly different (58)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/arterial-blood-gases> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Arterial Blood Gases" ;
    dmo:definition "Arterial blood gases are measured if oxygen saturation is less than 92%." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "arterial-blood-gases" ;
    dmo:name "Arterial Blood Gases" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Measure arterial blood gases and repeat chest radiograph if oxygen saturation less than 92%" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/bicarbonate> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:definition "should be assessed at or near the bedside using point of care testing" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:name "Bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Therefore glucose, ketones and electrolytes, including bicarbonate and venous pH, should be assessed at or near the bedside using point of care testing." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Bicarbonate and pH were not significantly different (58)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "At 12 hours check venous pH, bicarbonate, potassium, as well as blood ketones and glucose" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/bicarbonate-concentration> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Bicarbonate Concentration" ;
    dmo:definition "A bicarbonate concentration of <15.0 mmol/L is required for the diagnosis of DKA." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "bicarbonate-concentration" ;
    dmo:name "Bicarbonate Concentration" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The ‘A’ – a bicarbonate concentration of <15.0 mmol/L and/or venous pH <7.3" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "routinely checked using point of care testing" ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-glucose" ;
    dmo:loincCode "15540-2" ;
    dmo:name "Blood glucose" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Blood glucose is routinely checked using point of care testing, but portable ketone meters now also allow point of care testing of 3-beta-hydroxybutyrate, the main blood ketone." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The blood glucose may fall very rapidly as ketoacidosis is corrected and a common mistake is to allow the blood glucose to drop to hypoglycaemic levels." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Blood glucose to fall by at least 3mmol/L/hour" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose-concentration> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood Glucose Concentration" ;
    dmo:definition "A blood glucose concentration of >11.0 mmol/L is required for the diagnosis of DKA." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-glucose-concentration" ;
    dmo:name "Blood Glucose Concentration" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The ‘D’ – a blood glucose concentration of >11.0 mmol/L or known to have diabetes mellitus" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose-concentrations> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood Glucose Concentrations" ;
    dmo:definition "Measurement of blood glucose concentrations 24 hours per day – ideally using point of care testing meters" ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-glucose-concentrations" ;
    dmo:name "Blood Glucose Concentrations" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Every Acute Trust should have trained Health Care Workers available to measure blood glucose concentrations 24 hours per day – ideally using point of care testing meters" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketone> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood Ketone" ;
    dmo:definition "Measurement of ketone levels in blood to assess the response to treatment in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-ketone" ;
    dmo:name "Blood Ketone" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If blood ketone measurement is available and blood ketones are not falling by at least 0.5 mmol/L/hr, call a prescribing clinician to increase the insulin infusion rate by 1.0 unit/hr increments hourly until the ketones are falling at target rates (also check infusion**)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Check the blood ketone and glucose levels regularly" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketone-concentration> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood Ketone Concentration" ;
    dmo:definition "The concentration of ketones in the blood, which should be reduced by 0.5 mmol/L/hour during treatment of DKA." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-ketone-concentration" ;
    dmo:name "Blood Ketone Concentration" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Reduction of the blood ketone concentration by 0.5 mmol/L/hour" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketone-concentrations> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood Ketone Concentrations" ;
    dmo:definition "Measurement of blood ketone concentrations 24 hours per day – ideally using point of care testing meters" ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-ketone-concentrations" ;
    dmo:name "Blood Ketone Concentrations" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Every Acute Trust should have trained Health Care Workers available to measure blood ketone concentrations 24 hours per day – ideally using point of care testing meters" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketone-meters> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood ketone meters" ;
    dmo:definition "Used for point of care testing of blood ketones" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-ketone-meters" ;
    dmo:name "Blood ketone meters" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Blood ketone meters should be used for point of care testing" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "A measure of ketone bodies in the blood, used to assess the severity of diabetic ketoacidosis." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-ketones" ;
    dmo:loincCode "15541-0" ;
    dmo:name "Blood ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The presence of one or more of the following may indicate severe DKA: • Blood ketones over 6.0 mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Initial investigations should include: • Blood ketones" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Measure blood ketones and capillary glucose hourly (note: if meter reads “blood glucose over 20 mmol/L” or “Hi” venous blood should be sent to the laboratory hourly or measured using venous blood in a blood gas analyser until the point of care testing meter is within its QA range)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Resolution of DKA is defined as ketones less than 0.6 mmol/L and venous pH over 7.3" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Blood ketones to fall by at least 0.5mmol/L/hour" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "MONITORING HOURLY CBG, CAPILLARY KETONES, Urinary Output" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Blood Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "The concentration of glucose in capillary blood, which should be reduced by 3.0 mmol/L/hour during treatment of DKA." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-blood-glucose" ;
    dmo:loincCode "15540-2" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Blood Glucose" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "• capillary blood glucose above 11 mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Reduce capillary blood glucose by 3.0 mmol/L/hour" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "CBG not falling by at least 3 mmol/L/hr" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "1. Capillary blood glucose (CBG) 11.0mmol/L or known diabetes" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "MONITORING HOURLY CBG, CAPILLARY KETONES, Urinary Output" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-ketonaemia> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary blood ketonaemia" ;
    dmo:definition "Capillary blood ketonaemia on Trust approved ketone meter of ≥ 3 mmol/L" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-blood-ketonaemia" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary blood ketonaemia" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Capillary blood ketonaemia on Trust approved ketone meter of ≥ 3 mmol/L or" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Blood Ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "Capillary blood ketones are measured hourly until DKA is resolved." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-blood-ketones" ;
    dmo:loincCode "15541-0" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Blood Ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Check capillary blood ketone hourly until DKA resolved" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "2. Capillary blood ketones>3.0mmol/L or 2+ ketonuria" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "Measure blood ketones and capillary glucose hourly (note: if meter reads “blood glucose over 20 mmol/L” or “Hi” venous blood should be sent to the laboratory hourly or measured using venous blood in a blood gas analyser until the point of care testing meter is within its QA range)" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-glucose" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Glucose" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Measure blood ketones and capillary glucose hourly (note: if meter reads “blood glucose over 20 mmol/L” or “Hi” venous blood should be sent to the laboratory hourly or measured using venous blood in a blood gas analyser until the point of care testing meter is within its QA range)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-ketone-concentration> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Ketone Concentration" ;
    dmo:definition "A capillary or blood ketone concentration of >3.0 mmol/L is required for the diagnosis of DKA." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-ketone-concentration" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Ketone Concentration" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The ‘K’ – a capillary or blood ketone concentration of >3.0 mmol/L or significant ketonuria (2+ or more on standard urine sticks)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "Capillary ketones are measured hourly to assess response to treatment in DKA. A fall of at least 0.5 mmol/L/hr is expected." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-ketones" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "• capillary ketones above 3 mmol/L or urine ketones ++ or more" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Capillary ketones not falling by at least 0.5 mmol/L/hr" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/covid-19-testing> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "COVID-19 Testing" ;
    dmo:definition "Testing for SARS-CoV-2 infection, particularly in individuals without prior diabetes diagnosis, as infection may trigger DKA." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "covid-19-testing" ;
    dmo:name "COVID-19 Testing" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "NA" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "• COVID-19 testing - particularly in those not known to have a prior diagnosis of diabetes" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/creatinine> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Creatinine" ;
    dmo:definition "Creatinine is checked at 2 hours then 2 to 4 hourly until venous bicarbonate >15 mmol/L." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "creatinine" ;
    dmo:name "Creatinine" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Check creatinine, electrolyte and venous bicarbonate and pH at 2 hours then 2 to 4 hourly until venous bicarbonate >15 mmol/L" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/electrolyte> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Electrolyte" ;
    dmo:definition "Electrolyte is checked at 2 hours then 2 to 4 hourly until venous bicarbonate >15 mmol/L." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "electrolyte" ;
    dmo:name "Electrolyte" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Check creatinine, electrolyte and venous bicarbonate and pH at 2 hours then 2 to 4 hourly until venous bicarbonate >15 mmol/L" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/glasgow-coma-scale-score> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Glasgow Coma Scale Score" ;
    dmo:definition "A neurological scale used to assess the level of consciousness, where any deterioration should prompt urgent treatment and imaging." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "glasgow-coma-scale-score" ;
    dmo:name "Glasgow Coma Scale Score" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "However, any deterioration in Glasgow Coma Scale score should prompt urgent treatment and imaging." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "Monitored as part of the biochemical and metabolic parameters during DKA management." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "glucose" ;
    dmo:name "Glucose" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mg-per-dL" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "At 12 hours check venous pH, bicarbonate, potassium, as well as blood ketones and glucose" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Check the blood ketone and glucose levels regularly" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/hba1c> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "HbA1c" ;
    dmo:definition "A measure of average blood glucose levels over the past 2-3 months, used to assess long-term glycaemic control." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "hba1c" ;
    dmo:name "HbA1c" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/mol" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The person’s previous regimen should generally be re-started if their most recent HbA1c suggests acceptable level of control i.e. HbA1c 64 mmol/mmol (<8.0%)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_02_dka_guideline_with_qr_code_march_2023> .

